WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.101
A folder holds two log files.

2
00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:07.833
You type rm *.log to delete them.

3
00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:08.992
Simple enough.

4
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.297
But here's a question most people never ask.

5
00:00:12.297 --> 00:00:14.409
What does rm actually receive?

6
00:00:14.409 --> 00:00:16.943
Does rm even know what a star means?

7
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:21.677
Hold on to your guess.

8
00:00:21.677 --> 00:00:23.188
We'll come back to it.

9
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.286
Picture your first day in a big office building.

10
00:00:26.286 --> 00:00:31.157
At a small window in the lobby sits a clerk who reads each note you pass through.

11
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:33.270
The clerk doesn't do most jobs.

12
00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:38.121
It knows which worker does each one, calls them, and passes back what they say.

13
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.225
Let's map it.

14
00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:42.656
The terminal is the window: the app you type into.

15
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:49.311
The shell is the clerk: the program inside the window that reads your line and starts other programs.

16
00:00:49.311 --> 00:00:52.309
zsh on your Mac, usually bash on Linux servers.

17
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:56.851
A command is the worker, usually a program file on disk.

18
00:00:56.851 --> 00:01:00.474
ls, which lists files, is the file /usr/bin/ls.

19
00:01:00.474 --> 00:01:03.327
The clerk finds it through your PATH.

20
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:07.403
A few commands, like cd, are jobs the clerk does itself.

21
00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:09.200
Those are called builtins.

22
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:11.846
And here's the thing about this clerk.

23
00:01:11.846 --> 00:01:13.179
Brilliant at reading.

24
00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:14.513
Hopeless at guessing.

25
00:01:14.513 --> 00:01:16.735
It does exactly what the note says.

26
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:23.903
So let's watch the clerk read one note: ls -l -h server/var/log.

27
00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:27.701
Before any program starts, the clerk takes the note apart.

28
00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:30.653
The shell splits the line on spaces, into words.

29
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:33.433
The first word is the program to run.

30
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:39.284
Every other word becomes an argument: a plain piece of text, handed to that program.

31
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.459
Notice: the shell doesn't know what -l means.

32
00:01:42.459 --> 00:01:44.694
It only cuts the line into pieces.

33
00:01:44.694 --> 00:01:47.456
The program decides what each piece means.

34
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:51.606
By habit, words that start with a dash are options, or flags.

35
00:01:51.606 --> 00:01:54.513
Short ones are one letter and can be grouped.

36
00:01:54.513 --> 00:01:56.903
Long ones are words after two dashes.

37
00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:02.519
Now a file called my notes.txt, with a space in its name.

38
00:02:02.519 --> 00:02:04.399
You type ls my notes.txt.

39
00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:07.256
How many files does ls go looking for?

40
00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:10.792
Two.

41
00:02:10.792 --> 00:02:18.360
The clerk splits on that space, so ls asks for a file called my, and a file called notes.txt.

42
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.255
Two errors.

43
00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:21.368
The clerk did a perfect job.

44
00:02:21.368 --> 00:02:23.405
Of exactly what the note said.

45
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:28.106
Quote the name, or put a backslash before the space, and it stays one word.

46
00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:31.028
Better still, keep spaces out of your file names.

47
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:33.674
Now a different kind of note.

48
00:02:33.674 --> 00:02:36.952
Suppose it says: shred every report from October.

49
00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:43.553
The clerk walks to the drawer, writes down every October report's name, and hands the worker that list.

50
00:02:43.553 --> 00:02:46.033
The worker never hears the word October.

51
00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:49.898
A pattern for file names works the same way.

52
00:02:49.898 --> 00:02:51.890
In the lab, type echo *.log.

53
00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.448
The star matches any run of characters, even none.

54
00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.017
The shell splits the line, as before.

55
00:02:58.017 --> 00:02:59.669
But one word is a pattern.

56
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:05.261
So, before the program starts, the clerk reads the folder, and keeps the names that fit.

57
00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:08.567
Then it puts them, sorted, in place of the pattern.

58
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.015
The program never sees the pattern, only the names.

59
00:03:12.015 --> 00:03:14.979
And echo just prints the arguments it receives.

60
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.929
So, back to our opening question.

61
00:03:17.929 --> 00:03:19.583
What did rm receive?

62
00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:20.493
Not a star.

63
00:03:20.493 --> 00:03:21.733
Two file names.

64
00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:25.255
A pattern like this is called a glob, or a wildcard.

65
00:03:25.255 --> 00:03:27.468
Now you know who does the matching.

66
00:03:27.468 --> 00:03:29.112
The clerk, not the worker.

67
00:03:29.833 --> 00:03:34.106
Quotes switch globbing off: in quotes, echo prints the star itself.

68
00:03:34.106 --> 00:03:37.932
And a star skips hidden files, whose names start with a dot.

69
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:39.780
And when nothing matches?

70
00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:47.619
In bash the pattern passes on unchanged, as if the clerk found no October reports and handed over the words themselves.

71
00:03:47.833 --> 00:03:51.114
In zsh, the shell refuses to run the line at all.

72
00:03:51.114 --> 00:03:55.868
You will see the zsh way on your Mac, and the bash way on every server.

73
00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.171
Now the dangerous one, the shredder.

74
00:03:59.171 --> 00:04:04.314
You mean to delete one folder called old logs, with a space in its name.

75
00:04:04.314 --> 00:04:06.028
You type rm -r old logs.

76
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:08.655
You already know what the clerk does.

77
00:04:08.655 --> 00:04:12.647
It splits on the space, so rm gets two folders, old and logs.

78
00:04:12.867 --> 00:04:14.121
It deletes both.

79
00:04:14.121 --> 00:04:16.317
rm has no trash and no undo.

80
00:04:16.317 --> 00:04:18.983
The Mac's Trash belongs to Finder.

81
00:04:18.983 --> 00:04:21.021
rm just removes the names.

82
00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:23.257
So, the guardrails.

83
00:04:23.257 --> 00:04:25.663
Run pwd, to see where you are.

84
00:04:25.663 --> 00:04:29.753
Put echo in front, to see exactly what rm will get.

85
00:04:29.967 --> 00:04:34.170
While you learn, add -i, so rm asks before each file.

86
00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.628
And read any rm -rf line twice.

87
00:04:36.628 --> 00:04:39.800
The -f part switches off every question.

88
00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:41.319
rm has no undo.

89
00:04:41.319 --> 00:04:43.289
Look before you delete.

90
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.300
So that's the whole path of one note.

91
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:52.269
The clerk splits it on spaces, swaps patterns for real names, and hands the worker a plain list.

92
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.847
The program only ever sees what the clerk wrote down.

93
00:04:55.847 --> 00:04:59.511
So when in doubt, ask echo to read the clerk's list aloud.

94
00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:01.567
A quick check.

95
00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:05.267
A folder holds report.txt and .secret.txt.

96
00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:08.439
What does echo *.txt print, in bash?

97
00:05:11.667 --> 00:05:12.737
Just report.txt.

98
00:05:12.737 --> 00:05:20.363
A star at the start of a glob skips names that begin with a dot, so the clerk leaves the hidden file off the list.
